---
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<figure class="plate">
  <img src={u("/art/crikvenica-1943.webp")}
       alt="A constructed illustration: a narrow stone lane in an Adriatic coastal town at dusk, wet cobbles, shuttered windows, one lamp burning. Four armed men in mixed civilian and military dress walk a middle-aged man in a long coat and hat away up the lane, all seen from behind. A woman in a headscarf stands in a doorway to the right, her back to the viewer. The sea lies grey at the end of the lane."
       width="1700" height="927" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">
    <strong>Crikvenica, the winter of 1943.</strong> A constructed illustration, made for this archive by an image model
    working from a written brief. It is illustration and not evidence. Nobody in it has a face, and that is deliberate:
    an invented face put beside a real name would be a forgery, and this is the family's worst day.
    <br /><br />
    What the archive holds is a memory, not a document — that Anton Rudolf Defranceski, born at Klenovica on 20 January
    1890, was taken by the partisans, and that the family gives 1943 for it. The 1943 Crikvenica death register is closed
    under Croatia's hundred-year rule and open to a direct descendant on request; until a page from it is in front of
    this archive, the date and the manner stand as family lore and are marked as lore wherever they appear.
    <br /><br />
    So the picture shows nothing happening. No weapon is raised, no insignia is legible, no violence is depicted. A wet
    lane at dusk, the sea at the end of it, a woman in a doorway with her back turned, and men walking away. That is as
    much as anyone can honestly say.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .plate { margin: 0; }
  .plate img { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 820px; height: auto; border-radius: 2px; }
</style>
